{"evt":"note_created","id":"note-mqflngdgy51","workspace":"/Users/imyu/Desktop/zonoid","title":"Daemon workspace param must be an absolute filesystem path or notes silently fail to persist","summary":"CL-2 finding (2026-06-15), the load-bearing gotcha for any foreign-workspace KB use over HTTP: the daemon's `workspace` param on /overlay/note (write) and /search (read) must be an ABSOLUTE FILESYSTEM PATH, because the daemon does path.join(workspace, '.graph'). A bare/relative namespace string (e.g. 'cl-pytest-zonoid') makes the write return ok:true while the note is NEVER persisted, and /search returns nothing — silent data loss with no error. This is why the FeatureBench onboard workers keyed --workspace on the clone PATH and it worked. For the SWE-Bench-CL Zonoid memory adapter (bench/swe-bench-cl/zonoid_memory.py) each sequence uses an absolute path workspace. Other HTTP impedance for the adapter: write route is POST /overlay/note (NOT /record_decision — that's an MCP tool name, no HTTP route); pass force:true to bypass the 0.70-cosine dup guard or near-identical notes are admitted pending_dup and stay retrieval-invisible; /search is GET query-string (POST ignores query); cosine score is higher=better; first /search lazy-loads the embedder ~10-90s so call warm_up() once before the loop. POTENTIAL DAEMON FIX: reject or normalize a non-absolute workspace instead of silently dropping writes.","created_by":"main","valid_from":"2026-06-15T19:21:49.732Z","vec":[-0.15084980428218842,-0.022756127640604973,-0.05762873589992523,0.056356821209192276,0.006234145723283291,-0.07128851115703583,-0.013058115728199482,0.07899875193834305,0.007511165924370289,-0.014375739730894566,0.03629973903298378,0.03464265912771225,0.06959867477416992,-0.040470805019140244,-0.019037822261452675,0.013430913910269737,0.08990772068500519,-0.030348999425768852,0.008449611254036427,0.006258602254092693,0.1093878224492073,0.004296889994293451,0.09935850650072098,-0.04171792417764664,-0.0169729255139